{"task_id": "Mbpp/473", "solution": "def tuple_intersection(list_a, list_b):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the intersection of tuples in two lists, treating (a, b) and (b, a) as identical.\n    Each tuple's elements are sorted to create a canonical representation for comparison.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Helper to canonicalize a tuple by sorting its elements\n    def _canonicalize_tuple(tup):\n        if not isinstance(tup, (list, tuple)) or len(tup) != 2:\n            raise ValueError(\"All items must be 2-element tuples/lists\")\n        return tuple(sorted(tup))\n\n    # Canonicalize all tuples in list_a and convert to a set\n    canonical_set_a = {_canonicalize_tuple(t) for t in list_a}\n\n    # Canonicalize all tuples in list_b and convert to a set\n    canonical_set_b = {_canonicalize_tuple(t) for t in list_b}\n\n    # Find the intersection of the two sets of canonical tuples\n    return canonical_set_a.intersection(canonical_set_b)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/472", "solution": "def check_Consecutive(numbers):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given list contains consecutive numbers (order-agnostic, duplicates not allowed).\n    Returns True if numbers form a consecutive sequence like [3,4,5] or [5,3,4], False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return False\n\n    # Ensure all elements are integers\n    try:\n        nums = list(numbers)\n    except TypeError:\n        return False\n    for v in nums:\n        if not isinstance(v, int):\n            return False\n\n    # Duplicates not allowed by default\n    if len(set(nums)) != len(nums):\n        return False\n\n    sorted_nums = sorted(nums)\n    start = sorted_nums[0]\n    return sorted_nums == list(range(start, start + len(sorted_nums)))"}
